Here's how to set up relation resolvers with Prisma and TypeGraphQL:

```typescript
// gardener.ts
@ObjectType()
class Gardener {
  @Field()
  id: string;

  @Field()
  name: string;

  @Field(() => [Plot])
  plots: Plot[];
}

@Resolver(() => Gardener)
export class GardenerResolver {
  @FieldResolver(() => [Plot])
  async plots(
    @Root() gardener: Gardener,
    @Ctx() { prisma }: Context
  ): Promise<Plot[]> {
    return prisma.plot.findMany({
      where: { gardenerId: gardener.id }
    });
  }

  @Query(() => Gardener, { nullable: true })
  async gardener(
    @Arg("id") id: string,
    @Ctx() { prisma }: Context
  ) {
    return prisma.gardener.findUnique({
      where: { id }
    });
  }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `@FieldResolver` resolves the `plots` field on demand
- The `@Root()` parameter gives you the parent Gardener object
- Return type `Promise<Plot[]>` handles the one-to-many relationship
- DataLoader can optimize N+1 queries if you query many gardeners at once

If you want plots eagerly loaded, you can instead fetch them in your main query resolver with `include: { plots: true }`.

