Here's how to set up relation resolvers with Prisma and TypeGraphQL:

**1. Define your Prisma schema:**
```prisma
model Gardener {
  id        Int     @id @default(autoincrement())
  name      String
  plots     Plot[]  // one-to-many relation
}

model Plot {
  id         Int     @id @default(autoincrement())
  plantNotes String
  gardener   Gardener @relation(fields: [gardenerId], references: [id])
  gardenerId Int
}
```

**2. Generate Prisma Client and TypeGraphQL types:**
```bash
npx prisma generate
```

**3. Define ObjectType with relation resolver:**
```typescript
import { ObjectType, Field, Resolver, Query, Arg } from 'type-graphql';
import { Prisma } from '@prisma/client';

@ObjectType()
class Plot {
  @Field()
  id: number;

  @Field()
  plantNotes: string;
}

@ObjectType()
class Gardener {
  @Field()
  id: number;

  @Field()
  name: string;

  @Field(() => [Plot])
  plots: Plot[];
}

@Resolver(() => Gardener)
class GardenerResolver {
  @Query(() => Gardener)
  async gardener(
    @Arg('id') id: number,
    @Ctx() { prisma }: Context
  ) {
    return prisma.gardener.findUnique({
      where: { id },
      include: { plots: true } // Eagerly load plots
    });
  }
}
```

The `include: { plots: true }` in your query ensures the full plot list populates when you fetch a gardener.
